        "old_string": "      \"zoneBoundariesTestScope\": \"zone-boundaries.test.ts guards the entire 4-layer architecture DAG (engine → hooks → components → routes), making its conceptual scope global rather than zone-specific. It is classified in build-infrastructure because it is the runtime verification counterpart to eslint.config.ts — the static rule definition it validates. Both files form a cohesive pair: one defines the enforcement rules, the other proves they are correctly wired. This is not a misclassification; architecture enforcement tooling belongs with the build infrastructure that implements it.\"",
        "new_string": "      \"zoneBoundariesTestScope\": \"zone-boundaries.test.ts guards the entire 4-layer architecture DAG (engine → hooks → components → routes), making its conceptual scope global rather than zone-specific. It is classified in build-infrastructure because it is the runtime verification counterpart to eslint.config.ts — the static rule definition it validates. Both files form a cohesive pair: one defines the enforcement rules, the other proves they are correctly wired. This is not a misclassification; architecture enforcement tooling belongs with the build infrastructure that implements it.\",\n      \"worldSelectionUiCouplingConcentration\": \"Within world-selection-ui, worlds.tsx accounts for 3 of 4 cross-zone imports (all into game-engine via the hooks barrel), while RainbowArc.tsx has zero and WorldGrid.tsx has 1 (engine types). This concentration is structurally correct: worlds.tsx is the composition root that mounts SaveProvider, consumes hooks (useWorldSession, useRainbowEasterEgg), and passes data down to presentation components via props. RainbowArc.tsx and WorldGrid.tsx having zero or minimal engine imports is the desired outcome — it means they are pure presentation components receiving data through props rather than reaching into the engine directly. The zone's coupling score should not be interpreted as evenly distributed; the concentration in the composition root is the expected pattern for a route handler orchestrating a provider + hook + component tree.\""
      },
